{"title":"1961 AHSME Problems/Problem 27","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1961_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given two equiangular polygons <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b0256a78d58b6d5f7a53400b19e7747d2983fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/7/107c7c8e5a64d81ed24331e510b777b7d08345b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> with different numbers of sides; \neach angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b0256a78d58b6d5f7a53400b19e7747d2983fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> degrees and each angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/7/107c7c8e5a64d81ed24331e510b777b7d08345b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/9/61956d2f89769fa07ff4f1cae31eaf8074f7d726.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$kx$\" width=\"20\" height=\"12\" /> degrees, \nwhere <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is an integer greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. \nThe number of possibilities for the pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/8/2a86d5ce5060b7fa1d8bffd936243cec1f5fda08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/d/18d2c006157c35931b6d6abcb1e4028ceb0f4847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\infty\\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ \\text{finite, but greater than 2}\\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 2\\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 1\\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"529\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1961_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each angle in each equiangular polygon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/c/cdc70e5e3b672817c81bbaf869ee342ab0d1624b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{180(n-2)}{n} = 180 - \\frac{360}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"191\" height=\"38\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the number of sides.  As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> gets larger, each angle in the equiangular polygon approaches (but does not reach) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f0cd95f95238159d02dbcea0aca632c2928991c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />.  That means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/e/90ebfbcffe2b888acdc02ceba0b926a99179713a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$kx &lt; 180$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"71\" height=\"13\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13ce4d8e6f2a417ba1d8053dc70708ac0b168043.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; \\frac{180}{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"64\" height=\"37\" />.\n<br />\n<br />\nRecall that each angle in an equiangular triangle has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/d/08d5c45388de5c75af641c046d466de3592ffa98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />, each angle in an equiangular quadrilateral has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/7/6a7dfa8a2a90d521b2b03f5b82a1fb9f85dce88c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />, and so on.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/4/8e44ca2cae6be42148fe1b1fd8b5d55bd6b599bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/9/869e77446571ea0ff1dca3846375fccc5535213c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" />.  That means the only option available is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/c/bfc5a8f24764e16ec1b4fc73871ed11704a6b35f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 60$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" /> because an equiangular triangle is the only equiangular polygon to have acute angles.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/9/7f96a3de68cfb881f4b3cc002ec222bba93f1e16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/5/be5b3b129e6c6adf864160e50218a94f411be6a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; 60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" />, and there are no values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> that satisfy.  As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> increases, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/3/5d3f2af22cfffa65eacedb96e7ddd3a703d25f7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{180}{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"29\" height=\"37\" /> decreases even further, so we can for sure say that there is only 1 possibility for the pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/8/2a86d5ce5060b7fa1d8bffd936243cec1f5fda08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" />, which is answer choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/3/693c4044f8526a8179956e8ac7a3f2f9d61e2c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"43\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1961_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1961_AHSME\" title=\"1961 AHSME\">1961 AHSC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1961_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1961 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1961_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1961 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=44&amp;year=1961\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n